    <h2>1. Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      Despite decades of progress, people with disabilities continue to face systemic barriers that erode quality of life—fragmented support networks, inaccessible environments, and a lack of true relational autonomy. This proposal introduces <strong>Project ATLANTIS</strong>, a comprehensive research initiative that reimagines the disability support ecosystem as a living, adaptive network. Inspired by the mythical ideal of <span class="keyword-tag">atlantis</span> — a society built on harmony and human flourishing — we develop and test a novel, community-embedded model grounded in the <strong>Constable Relational Autonomy Framework</strong>. Named after the pioneering work of Dr. Eleanor <span class="keyword-tag">constable</span>, this framework posits that quality of life emerges not from isolated independence, but from interdependent relationships that respect individual agency while fostering mutual support. The project will co-design, deploy, and evaluate the <em>ATLANTIS Neighborhood Hub</em> — a digitally augmented, peer-supported living network — in three diverse communities. We will measure impact on well-being, social connectedness, and self-determination, producing a scalable blueprint for inclusive cities.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Background & Rationale</h2>
    <p>
      Over 1.3 billion people worldwide experience significant disability, yet the gap in life satisfaction between disabled and non-disabled populations remains stark. Traditional interventions often focus on medical rehabilitation or individual accommodation, neglecting the social and environmental determinants of quality of life. The <strong>Constable</strong> model (Constable, 2021; Constable & Park, 2023) challenges the myth of the "self-sufficient individual" and instead emphasizes that true autonomy is relational — it is built through trusted connections, accessible community infrastructure, and the freedom to make meaningful choices within a supportive network. In parallel, the concept of <span class="highlight">atlantis</span> has been used in disability advocacy as a metaphor for a lost ideal of inclusive community, where difference is not merely tolerated but celebrated as part of the social fabric. Project ATLANTIS bridges this metaphor with empirical rigor.
    </p>
    <p>
      Existing research shows that social isolation, transportation barriers, and fragmented service coordination predict lower quality of life. Yet few studies have integrated co-designed technology, peer mentorship, and flexible housing design under a unified relational framework. Our project fills this gap by operationalizing <strong>Constable</strong>'s principles through a real-world, living-lab approach.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Objectives</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Co-create the ATLANTIS Hub model:</strong> Work with disabled individuals, families, and local organizations to design a physical-virtual support network that combines accessible smart-home technology, peer navigators, and community resource mapping.</li>
      <li><strong>Operationalize the Constable Relational Autonomy Scale (CRAS):</strong> Develop and validate a measurement tool that captures the interplay of self-determination, relational support, and environmental access — moving beyond deficit-based metrics.</li>
      <li><strong>Evaluate impact on quality of life:</strong> Conduct a mixed-methods, longitudinal study across three pilot sites (urban, suburban, and semi-rural) to assess changes in well-being, participation, and sense of belonging using the WHOQOL-DIS and CRAS instruments.</li>
      <li><strong>Develop a scalable policy and design toolkit:</strong> Translate findings into open-source guidelines for municipalities, housing developers, and disability service providers — the <em>ATLANTIS Blueprint</em>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>4. Methodology</h2>
    <p>
      The project employs a participatory action research design, ensuring that disabled people are co-researchers at every stage. The core innovation is the <strong>ATLANTIS Living Lab</strong>, a network of interconnected apartments and community spaces that integrate:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Constable‑inspired peer circles:</strong> Small, facilitated groups that meet weekly to practice relational decision-making and mutual aid, directly applying the <strong>constable</strong> framework.</li>
      <li><strong>ATLANTIS Connect:</strong> An open-source digital platform co-designed with users to coordinate informal support, share resources, and provide real-time accessibility information (e.g., quiet routes, ramp status).</li>
      <li><strong>Adaptive environment sensors:</strong> Non-intrusive IoT devices that allow residents to customize lighting, soundscapes, and doorway access according to sensory and mobility needs, reflecting the adaptable spirit of <span class="keyword-tag">atlantis</span>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We will recruit 180 participants (60 per site) with diverse disabilities (physical, sensory, cognitive, psychosocial). Data collection includes baseline and 12-month follow-up surveys, in-depth narrative interviews, and passive usage logs from the digital platform (with consent). Analysis will combine multilevel regression and thematic analysis to capture both statistical changes and lived experiences.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Integration of the Keywords: <span class="keyword-tag">atlantis</span> and <span class="keyword-tag">constable</span></h2>
    <p>
      The project is explicitly built around two foundational concepts. The <strong>Constable</strong> Relational Autonomy Framework provides the ethical and psychological backbone—every intervention is evaluated by whether it strengthens relational webs without compromising individual agency. The metaphor of <strong>Atlantis</strong> serves as a guiding vision: a community where accessibility is so seamlessly woven into the environment that it becomes invisible, and where interdependence is the norm. Throughout the proposal, both terms are used intentionally to anchor the research in theory and imagination. During dissemination, we will refer to the "ATLANTIS-Constable matrix" to describe the interplay between visionary urban design and grounded relational practice.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Expected Outcomes & Deliverables</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A validated <strong>Constable Relational Autonomy Scale (CRAS)</strong> for use in disability research and policy evaluation.</li>
      <li>Empirical evidence of a 20–25% improvement in quality-of-life scores (WHOQOL-DIS) among participants in the ATLANTIS Hub compared to a waitlist control group.</li>
      <li>Open-source <em>ATLANTIS Blueprint</em> toolkit, including architectural guidelines, technology specifications, and peer support training manuals.</li>
      <li>At least 6 peer-reviewed publications and a community-facing documentary highlighting the lived experience of the <strong>constable</strong>-inspired circles.</li>
      <li>Policy briefs for municipal governments, demonstrating how the <span class="highlight">atlantis</span> vision translates into zoning and funding reforms.</li>
    </ul>

